\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Quiz 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Explain the basic idea of the maximum likelihood estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
In estimation problems, we do not know the distribution parameters, but
 we have a set of observations.
 Maximum likelihood estimation finds the distribution parameter that would
 have generated the sample on hand most likely.
 Assuming we have a random sample, so the observations are independent,
 the likelihood function and the log-likelihood function are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{t=1}^{n}}f(y_{t};\theta)\\
l & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ln\left[f(y_{t};\theta)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the marginal pdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the joint pdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1},\ldots,y_{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Given a sample, we want to maximize the log-likelihood function with respect
 to the parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a standard linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u$
\end_inset

, which satisfies the Gauss-Markov assumptions and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\sim i.i.d.N(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Write the log likelihood function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the first order conditions and solve for the unknown parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Are the estimators unbiased? Just explain why or why not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the maximum value of the log likelihood function.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
The log likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln L=-\frac{N}{2}\ln(2\pi)-\frac{N}{2}\ln(\sigma^{2})-\frac{(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)}{2\sigma^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

The first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial\ln L}{\partial\beta} & = & -\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\left(-2X^{\prime}y+2X^{\prime}X\beta\right)=0\\
\hat{\beta} & = & (X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial\ln L}{\partial\sigma^{2}} & = & -\frac{N}{2}\frac{1}{\sigma^{2}}+\frac{(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)}{2\sigma^{4}}=0\\
\hat{\sigma}^{2} & = & \frac{(y-X\hat{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\hat{\beta})}{N}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the Gauss-Markove assumptions are satisfied we know 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is unbiased.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is biased because the unbiased estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{(y-X\hat{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\hat{\beta})}{N-K}
\]

\end_inset

The maximum vaue of the log-likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln L=-\frac{N}{2}\ln(2\pi)-\frac{N}{2}\ln(\hat{\sigma}^{2})-\frac{N}{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

